-- title:
--   Pessimism–width decomposition of Thompson sampling's expected per-period regret (§8.1.2, display after (8.4))
-- statement:
--   Work on a probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mathbb P)$. A random parameter $\theta$ takes values in a measurable space $\Theta$, the disturbances $w_1,w_2,\dots$ are i.i.d. and independent of $\theta$, and in period $t\ge1$ the agent takes an action $x_t$ in a measurable space $\mathcal X$ (finite or infinite) and observes the outcome $y_t=g(x_t,\theta,w_t)$, earning the reward $r(y_t)$. The mean reward is $\mu(x,\vartheta)=\mathbb E[r(g(x,\vartheta,w_1))]$, the history $H_{t-1}=((x_1,y_1),\dots,(x_{t-1},y_{t-1}))$ is identified with the $\sigma$-algebra it generates, and Thompson sampling chooses $x_t=f(\hat\theta_t)$, where $\hat\theta_t$ is a fresh sample from the posterior law of $\theta$ given $H_{t-1}$ and $f$ is a measurable selector of $\arg\max_{x}\mu(x,\cdot)$. The optimal action is $x^*=f(\theta)\in\arg\max_x\mu(x,\theta)$.
--
--   Let $t\ge1$ and let $U_t:\Omega\times\mathcal X\to\mathbb R$ be any function determined by the history $H_{t-1}$ (measurable for $H_{t-1}\otimes\mathcal B(\mathcal X)$) such that $\mu(x^*,\theta)$, $\mu(x_t,\theta)$, $U_t(x_t)$ and $U_t(x^*)$ are integrable. Then
--
--   $$\begin{aligned}\mathbb E[\mu(x^*,\theta)-\mu(x_t,\theta)]&=\mathbb E[\mu(x^*,\theta)-U_t(x_t)]+\mathbb E[U_t(x_t)-\mu(x_t,\theta)]\\&=\underbrace{\mathbb E[\mu(x^*,\theta)-U_t(x^*)]}_{\text{pessimism}}+\underbrace{\mathbb E[U_t(x_t)-\mu(x_t,\theta)]}_{\text{width}}.\end{aligned}$$
--
--   Both equalities are asserted. The expected regret of Thompson sampling in period $t$ thus splits into a pessimism term and a width term for any history-determined $U_t$, although $U_t$ plays no role in the algorithm; when $U_t$ is an upper confidence bound, regret bounds for UCB algorithms become Bayesian regret bounds for Thompson sampling.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mu$ is `meanReward P w g r`, $x^*$ is `f (θ ω)`, $x_t$ is `xs t ω`, $U_t(x)$ is `U ω x`, and $H_{t-1}$ is `history g θ w xs t`.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: x* ∈ argmax_x µ(x, θ), and TS's line 6 xt ← argmax_x µ(x, θ̂) (tie-breaking unspecified) → one measurable selector f : Θ → X of argmax_x µ(x, ·), with x* = f(θ) and x_t = f(θ̂_t); reason: the paper's claim that TS samples x_t from the posterior of x* presupposes the same rule; with different tie-breaking the identity is false (θ constant, X = {1, 2}, µ ≡ 0, x* = 1, x_t = 2, U_t(1) = 0, U_t(2) = 1 gives 0 = 0 + 1)
--   - generalization: a run of Algorithm 4 (sample θ̂ from the current posterior p, act greedily, update p by Bayes' rule) → IsThompsonRun: P(θ̂_t ∈ A | H_{t−1}) = P(θ ∈ A | H_{t−1}) a.s. for every measurable A, and θ̂_t conditionally independent of (θ, w) given H_{t−1}, stated set-wise; reason: every run of Algorithm 4 with a measurable tie-breaking rule satisfies these clauses (iterating the Bayes update gives the posterior given H_{t−1}); the clauses also admit samples that depend on earlier samples beyond H_{t−1}, and need no regular conditional distribution or standard-Borel space
--   - generalization: µ(x, θ) = E[r(g(x, θ, w_t)) | θ], defined when the expectation exists → meanReward = ∫ r(g(x, ϑ, v)) d(law of w_1)(v), with no integrability assumed (Bochner value 0 where r ∘ g is not integrable); reason: by independence and identical distribution this is a version of the paper's µ wherever the paper's µ is defined; the statement holds for any values of µ, so it specializes to the paper's case
--   - encoding: period t ∈ N and history H_{t−1} = ((x_1, y_1), …, (x_{t−1}, y_{t−1})) → t : ℕ with 1 ≤ t; H_{t−1} is the σ-algebra history g θ w xs t generated by (x_s, g(x_s, θ, w_s)) for 1 ≤ s < t; reason: 1-based periods without natural-number subtraction; conditioning on the history is conditioning on the σ-algebra it generates
--   - encoding: θ a random variable, (w_t) i.i.d. and independent of θ, g and r known functions → IsBayesModel: θ, w_t measurable, g jointly measurable, r measurable, (w_{i+1})_i iIndepFun and IdentDistrib, θ independent of the whole sequence; f measurable; reason: measurability is the standing convention implicit in the paper's random variables and expectations
--   - reading: U_t is determined by the history H_{t−1} → (ω, x) ↦ U ω x is measurable for the product σ-algebra H_{t−1} ⊗ 𝓑(X); reason: U_t is a random function of x fixed by the history; joint measurability makes ω ↦ U_t(x_t)(ω) and ω ↦ U_t(x*)(ω) random variables, which measurability for each fixed x does not
--   - encoding: E[µ(x*, θ) − µ(x_t, θ)], E[µ(x*, θ) − U_t(x_t)], E[U_t(x_t) − µ(x_t, θ)], E[µ(x*, θ) − U_t(x*)] (assumed to exist) → Integrable hypotheses for µ(x*, θ), µ(x_t, θ), U_t(x_t), U_t(x*); reason: the display is derived from (8.4), which presupposes E[U_t(x_t)] and E[U_t(x*)]; given these, the four expectations of differences exist iff the four terms are integrable, so the hypotheses exclude only the cases where the paper's expectations are undefined (Bochner integrals are 0 there)
-- source:
--   Russo, Van Roy, Kazerouni, Osband, Wen, A Tutorial on Thompson Sampling, Found. Trends Mach. Learn. 11(1) (2018), doi:10.1561/2200000070, §8.1.2, display following (8.4), p. 73 (PDF p. 76): "From Equation (8.4), it follows that E[µ(x∗, θ) − µ(xt, θ)] = E[µ(x∗, θ) − Ut(xt)] + E[Ut(xt) − µ(xt, θ)] = E[µ(x∗, θ) − Ut(x∗)] (pessimism) + E[Ut(xt) − µ(xt, θ)] (width)."

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ThompsonTutorial_Regret_meanReward
import Definitions.Def_ThompsonTutorial_Regret_IsBayesModel
import Definitions.Def_ThompsonTutorial_Regret_history
import Definitions.Def_ThompsonTutorial_Regret_IsThompsonRun
open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace ThompsonTutorial.Regret

/-- **Pessimism–width decomposition of Thompson sampling's expected per-period regret.**
Russo, Van Roy, Kazerouni, Osband, Wen, *A Tutorial on Thompson Sampling*, Found. Trends Mach.
Learn. 11(1) (2018), §8.1.2, display following (8.4), p. 73 (PDF p. 76): "From Equation (8.4),
it follows that E[µ(x∗, θ) − µ(xt, θ)] = E[µ(x∗, θ) − Ut(xt)] + E[Ut(xt) − µ(xt, θ)]
= E[µ(x∗, θ) − Ut(x∗)] (pessimism) + E[Ut(xt) − µ(xt, θ)] (width)."

In the Bayesian model of §8.1.2 (`IsBayesModel`), let `x_t` be the actions of Thompson sampling
(Algorithm 4, `IsThompsonRun`) with argmax selector `f`, let `x* = f(θ)` be the optimal action
(`x* ∈ argmax_x µ(x, θ)`), and let `t ≥ 1`. For every function `U : Ω → X → ℝ` (`U ω x` is
`U_t(x)`) determined by the history `H_{t−1}` — jointly measurable for `H_{t−1} ⊗ 𝓑(X)` — such
that the four expectations `E[µ(x*, θ)]`, `E[µ(x_t, θ)]`, `E[U_t(x_t)]`, `E[U_t(x*)]` exist, both
equalities of the display hold.

**Formalization Note.** `µ` is `meanReward P w g r`; `H_{t−1}` is `history g θ w xs t` (1-based
periods). The optimal action and Thompson sampling's argmax use the same selector `f`; with
different tie-breaking the identity fails (a constant `θ`, `X = {1, 2}`, `µ ≡ 0`, `x* = 1`,
`x_t = 2`, `U_t(1) = 0`, `U_t(2) = 1` gives `0 = 0 + 1`). The integrability hypotheses only exclude
the cases where the paper's expectations are undefined. -/
theorem ts_regret_pessimism_width_decomposition {Ω Θ X Y W : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]
    [MeasurableSpace Θ] [MeasurableSpace X] [MeasurableSpace Y] [MeasurableSpace W]
    (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (θ : Ω → Θ) (w : ℕ → Ω → W)
    (g : X → Θ → W → Y) (r : Y → ℝ) (f : Θ → X) (θhat : ℕ → Ω → Θ) (xs : ℕ → Ω → X)
    (hM : IsBayesModel P θ w g r) (hTS : IsThompsonRun P θ w g r f θhat xs)
    (t : ℕ) (ht : 1 ≤ t) (U : Ω → X → ℝ)
    (hU : Measurable[(history g θ w xs t).prod ‹MeasurableSpace X›] (Function.uncurry U))
    (hmustar : Integrable (fun ω => meanReward P w g r (f (θ ω)) (θ ω)) P)
    (hmux : Integrable (fun ω => meanReward P w g r (xs t ω) (θ ω)) P)
    (hUx : Integrable (fun ω => U ω (xs t ω)) P)
    (hUstar : Integrable (fun ω => U ω (f (θ ω))) P) :
    ∫ ω, (meanReward P w g r (f (θ ω)) (θ ω) - meanReward P w g r (xs t ω) (θ ω)) ∂P
        = ∫ ω, (meanReward P w g r (f (θ ω)) (θ ω) - U ω (xs t ω)) ∂P
          + ∫ ω, (U ω (xs t ω) - meanReward P w g r (xs t ω) (θ ω)) ∂P ∧
    ∫ ω, (meanReward P w g r (f (θ ω)) (θ ω) - U ω (xs t ω)) ∂P
          + ∫ ω, (U ω (xs t ω) - meanReward P w g r (xs t ω) (θ ω)) ∂P
        = ∫ ω, (meanReward P w g r (f (θ ω)) (θ ω) - U ω (f (θ ω))) ∂P
          + ∫ ω, (U ω (xs t ω) - meanReward P w g r (xs t ω) (θ ω)) ∂P := by sorry

end ThompsonTutorial.Regret
